---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// No hero artwork, on purpose. A course arguing that confident presentation is
// not evidence should not open with a decorative photograph of a lecture
// theatre it does not have.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    The step in between — where a distribution becomes advice for a particular
    person — is never shown, because it usually has not been taken. Ten thousand steps was the name of a
    1965 pedometer. The twenty-five minute block was one student with a kitchen
    timer who tested it on nobody but himself.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the unit of design here is one day: not a habit, not a goal, not a life. Twenty-four
    hours, taken apart and rebuilt, twelve times.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will actually do</h2>
  <p>
    Record one ordinary day and change nothing about it. Decide what "tired"
    means precisely enough to write down. Measure one night with two instruments
    and watch them disagree. Take something away and put it back. Try to
    conceal a condition from yourself, and mostly fail. Run an experiment
    twice, ten weeks apart, and get two different answers.
  </p>

  <p>
    You will hand in a pre-registration before you collect any data, a portfolio
    of protocols, one full report, and one replication. You are marked on the
    quality of your reasoning and never on whether your day improved — see the
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies</a> for why that distinction is not
    negotiable here.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone, in first year, with no background in statistics or physiology. Every
    technical term is explained where you first meet it and collected in the
    <a href={withBase("/glossary/")}>glossary</a>. If you find writing here that only
    somebody already trained could follow, that is a fault in the course and we
    would like to know.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not a wellbeing course and it will not make your days better. It is a
    methods course that happens to use your own life as the material, which is
    the only material available at this sample size.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you enrol</h2>
  <p>
    Four of the twelve labs ask you to change something about your sleep, your
    eating or your caffeine. Each carries a stopping rule, a limit on what the
    exercise must never become, and an alternative protocol that teaches the
    same method without asking anything of your body. Taking the alternative
    costs no marks and needs no reason. All of it is printed on the week's own
    page, not buried in a handbook.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Schedule" href="/schedule/">
      <p>Every week's lecture, lab and due date in one table.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces, what each is worth, and what is being judged.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Glossary" href="/glossary/">
      <p>Every technical word the course uses, in plain language.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who runs which lab, and who to ask first.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
